      "Ongoing Rules for Broker-Dealers and Agents",
      "Once a firm or person is registered, they must follow many rules to keep that registration.\n\nBroker-dealers must renew their registration every year. They must also:\n• Net capital: Keep meeting net capital requirements. The Administrator cannot require more net capital than the SEC does.\n• Financial reports: File regular reports on the firm's financial condition. A state cannot ask for reports the SEC does not require. If a filed report becomes wrong, the firm must promptly file an update. The Administrator may require these reports to be sent to clients.\n• Form BD: Promptly amend Form BD if any of its information changes.\n• Records: Keep client trade records and marketing materials for three years, the first two in an easy-to-reach place. Keep client account records for six years. These rules still apply if the firm goes out of business.\n• Audits: Allow audits or inspections by state and federal regulators at any time, and give access to any record the regulators find useful.\n• Bonds: Post a bond or insurance if the state requires it because the firm holds customer money or securities or has discretion over accounts.\n\nAgents have fewer ongoing rules than their firms. But each agent must make sure the firm keeps their Form U4 current, renews their registration every year, and pays the renewal fee. Agents must also meet FINRA continuing education rules.\n\nCustomers may ask for copies of any information they are entitled to at any time, such as financial statements or Form CRS.",
