      "s63-c3-material",
      "Material Facts and Product Disclosures",
      "Firms and professionals must tell clients about their business practices, procedures, and client relationships. They share this when an account is opened and after that. Some disclosures are also needed when making recommendations.\n\nAgents and IARs must disclose all material facts to clients and prospects. Material facts are facts a reasonable person would want or need to know to make an investment decision.\n\nMaterial facts cannot be left out or misstated. Misrepresentation is describing your qualifications, services, or an investment inaccurately. Omission is leaving out something a reasonable person would want to know. Facts that are not material may be left out.\n\nImportant client disclosures include:\n• Whether research or recommendations came from a third party.\n• How the professional is paid.\n• Material conflicts of interest.\n• Whether a broker-dealer is owned by, tied to, or under common control with an issuer.\n• The people and firms involved in the trade or advice.\n• The securities or advice involved.\n• The professional's role in the transaction.\n\nBroker-dealers must respond to formal written requests and complaints. An agent must bring any written complaint to the broker-dealer.",
