      "Talking About Investment Companies",
      "Investment companies include unit investment trusts, closed-end funds, open-end funds (mutual funds), and face-amount certificates. When discussing them, professionals:\n• Must disclose all sales charges and possible discounts, such as breakpoints, letters of intent, and rights of accumulation.\n• Cannot call a fund \"no load\" if it has a sales charge or a 12b-1 fee above 0.25%.\n• Must make sure the share class they recommend is suitable for the investor.\n• Should not recommend buying several funds with the same objective.\n• Must give the fund's average annual return for one-, five-, and ten-year periods if they want to mention its current income.\n• Must explain how the fund's risks differ from those of any other investment they recommend.\n• May not predict future performance.\n• May not sell dividends, which means urging a customer to buy a fund just before it pays a dividend.\n• Must describe all material facts and risks.\n• Must not mark up or highlight parts of the fund prospectus.",
